###
    ███      ████████  ███   ███  ████████  ████████   
    ███      ███        ███ ███   ███       ███   ███  
    ███      ███████     █████    ███████   ███████    
    ███      ███        ███ ███   ███       ███   ███  
    ███████  ████████  ███   ███  ████████  ███   ███  
###

use ./utils ▪ kc
use ..      ◆ kode lexer

# tokenize asks every pattern the same question at every token: does this
# match *here*, at the cursor. the answer it keeps is `match.index === 0`,
# so a pattern that matches further along is not a match - it is a scan of
# the whole rest of the file whose result gets thrown away. 25 of those per
# token is what made tokenize quadratic, and tokenize was 64% of a build.
#
# `y` asks the question where it gets asked. without it a build spends most
# of its time inside patterns that cannot match - a triple quote, a ▸, a / -
# scanning past the cursor for something they are never going to be given.
#
# the lookarounds are unaffected, and not by luck. the loop slices the text
# as it goes, so a lookbehind has never had anything to its left to look at
# and `(?<!\d\s*)` could never fail; anchoring does not change that. the
# forward lookahead is a forward lookahead either way.

▸ lexer

    ▸ anchored

        # the invariant. nothing else here can catch it going back, and
        # nothing but a benchmark would notice it being gone.

        lx = new lexer new kode header:false

        for pair in lx.regs

            pair[1].sticky ▸ true

    ▸ lookarounds

        # the three patterns that carry a lookaround, checked at the cursor
        # on the cases where the lookahead is what decides the token.
        # basics.kode and array.kode carry the wider corpus.

        # regex: (?<!\d\s*)\/...\/[gimsuy]*(?!\s*\d)

        kc '7/7'          ▸ '7 / 7'           # nothing to close it
        kc 'n/2/7'        ▸ 'n / 2 / 7'       # the lookahead turns /2/ away
        kc 'a = /re/ b'   ▸ 'a = /re/\nb'     # and here it does not

        # dots: (?<!\.)\.\.\.?(?!\.)

        kc '[1..2]'       ▸ ';[1,2]'
        kc '[1...2]'      ▸ ';[1]'

        # test: (?<!●)▸[\s\n]+  -  a ▸ opens a section, a ●▸ is a profile

        kc '●▸ foo\nbar'  ▸ "_k_.profile('foo')\nbar"